WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
And ai that can grab your mouse and keyboard and use your computer like a superhuman helper just arrived. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 159. Here's something that's going to change how you handle homework, group projects, and the boring clicks in between. Open ai, the lab that makes the ChatGPT chat app, has released a system named Astra. ChatGPT is the tool where you type a question and get a written answer back. Astra does not stop at answers.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:56.940
It treats the pixels on your laptop as a real desk it can reach into. Picture a lightning fast intern parked in the chair next to yours. You name a goal, and that intern opens apps, rolls down pages, and types into boxes. You do not have to narrate every tap. Until now, most chat systems only talked in words. They were like a classmate who explains the assignment but never picks up the pencil. Astra picks up the pencil.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:13.760
It works inside the school and hobby programs you already keep on your machine. If your group is building a presentation, it could collect facts from several sites while you shape the story. That is the jump from a friend who gives advice to a friend who actually does a slice of the work with you.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:41.620
Homework, art projects, and dull daily chores on a computer could all start to feel different. Artificial intelligence, or AI, is sliding from tell me things toward go handle this part. Right now, Astra lives inside certain ChatGPT plans. If your plan includes it, hand it one clear job. Ask it to open your browser and find the latest news about space. Then watch it map each click and keystroke before it moves.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:02:00.720
The more you practice with chained goals, the faster you'll see where this helper shines and where it still needs you. Astra also shows strong spatial awareness. That just means it can tell where things sit on a screen and how big they are. It is like knowing the lamp is on your left and the window is on your right without thinking about it.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:11.360
Older systems often got lost when a layout grew or a 3D scene got large. 3D means objects that have height, width, and depth, the way a game world does.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:23.200
This one staves steady because it works with real sizes inside actual programs, instead of guessing from sentences alone. That footing helps it finish jobs that used to stump language models.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:37.220
A language model is an AI that predicts the next word, like a very fancy autocomplete. Those older models slipped when they tried to write code or make bigger pictures, because they were imagining the page instead of touching it.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:47.200
If you care about design or game creation, a helper like this could one day assemble 3D characters or drag clips along a video timeline right on the display.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:54.000
It also opens a door for anyone who wants repetitive computer work done without first learning a pile of commands.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:04.300
If you have a supported plan, try a chained request. Ask it to create a new folder, move your recent downloads into it, and open that folder in a new window.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:15.260
Then watch how it lines up the steps. Another shift is even closer to home. You know the notes app on your phone, the one you open to jot a reminder without launching a giant spreadsheet?

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:30.060
People are starting to build AI helpers the same way. Instead of waiting for a huge company to ship one massive program that tries to do everything for everyone, they make a small personal version that solves one tiny problem they actually care about.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:38.660
One helper might sort study notes. Another might spit out quick ideas for a story. A third might nudge you about homework in your own wording.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:51.660
Compare that to a kitchen. Buying one enormous machine that slices blends and toasts and does each job kind of badly, versus keeping three simple gadgets that each do one task perfectly for you.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:04:01.000
These smaller helpers do not need years of engineering or giant teams. Students and hobbyists can shape their own, tune to how they learn or create.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.200
AI then stops feeling like something only big tech owns and starts feeling like something you can bend around your life.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:23.100
Ethan Malik, who studies how people use these tools at work in school, puts it in a catchy way. He says everyone will likely build their own small S software, while only specialists will take on the big S kind that runs entire companies.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:31.020
Small S just means a little custom helper for you. Big S means the heavy programs that keep a whole business standing.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:39.900
That split drops the barrier for regular users who only want to match for their exact routine. Walk through how a tiny one could come together.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:46.680
First, you start with a large AI that already knows a lot, like a student who has aced every subject.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:05:00.420
Second, you point it at one narrow job, not a hundred jobs. Third, you do not rebuild the engine. You add a thin layer of instructions, the way you add a shortcut on your home screen instead of buying a new phone.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:13.520
Fourth, you get a helper that fits your habits, the way a playlist only plays your songs. A student could make a quick tool that turns lecture notes into flashcards without needing to understand the full model underneath.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:23.080
Over time, helpers like that could feel as ordinary as a custom wallpaper or a playlist. Each one tuned to one person's subjects and quirks.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:34.540
Once you see the pattern, making a tiny helper stops feeling like a job for a giant lab. And thropic, the company behind the clawed chatbot, just put clawed inside Apple CarPlay.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:45.420
Clawed is a chat AI you usually talk to on a phone or laptop. Apple CarPlay is the screen in many cars that mirrors your iPhone so you can use apps without holding the phone.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:54.940
You can talk to clawed hands-free while driving or riding. It feels like a sharp passenger who can answer questions, help plan a route or bounce ideas around.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:06:08.000
Anyone with a compatible car and an iPhone can try it when the car is connected. Go to your CarPlay screen, say, hey Siri, open Clawed, and then ask it to help you remember three fun facts about dinosaurs.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:16.360
Siri is the voice assistant already built into iPhones. You may need a parents help to sign up for the Clawed app if you are under 18.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:23.640
The hands-free setup keeps your eyes on the road while you still get to explore an idea or grab a quick answer on a commute.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.580
This is AI sliding into everyday spaces like cars, where typing is not an option.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.420
Meta, the company behind Instagram and Facebook, released a new real-time audio model.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:41.080
Real-time means it keeps up as you speak instead of making you wait for a full recording to finish.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:52.320
An audio model is AI trained to make sense of sound. This one turns spoken words into text almost instantly, and it figures out who is talking and when a sentence ends.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:07:04.020
It works in tiny chunks of 80 milliseconds. A millisecond is 1,000th of a second, so 80 of them pass faster than most people notice, like the blink you take before you catch a tossed ball.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:10.180
That kind of speed could power future glasses or earbuds that quietly understand talk around you.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:15.280
If you want a taste of similar voice tech today, open the Gemini app on your phone.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:22.420
Gemini is Google's chat AI. Speak a long sentence and watch how quickly your words become text you can edit.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:28.140
The more naturally you talk, the more you'll feel why live listening matters for future assistants.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:36.180
The model also spots sentence boundaries, so it knows when one person stops and another starts, which helps in group conversations.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:43.320
That mix of speed and accuracy is a building block for gadgets that stay ready to listen without constant button presses.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:50.420
Open AI says it has built an automated AI research intern that can help with parts of scientific work.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.160
The company hopes to grow that intern into a full automated researcher within the next couple of years.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:03.520
The target sits on a path toward an even stronger automated AI researcher expected by March 2028.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:09.160
Writers are pushing back after publishers and agents claimed parts of a settlement payment from Anthropic.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:14.120
Sharing money gets messy when AI learns from published books and articles.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.020
The rules around who owns creative work are still being written.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.140
Authors argue that publishers appear to be claiming more than their fair share of the payments.

